{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claim requires that a genuinely reliable panel reject a correct outcome when a mandatory 'stop-condition' is met. Counterexample: suppose the checklist is correctly calibrated and the stop-condition is 'mandatory' not because it tracks the outcome but because it is a process safeguard (e.g., 'stop if documentation is missing'). If the objective outcome is nonetheless independently verifiable as correct through other means, the panel that rejects the correct answer solely because the stop-condition fired is less reliable, not more. Genuine reliability for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes is measured against the outcome, not against adherence to a stop-condition that may be over-conservative. Thus requiring rejection of a correct outcome is an anti-reliability criterion.","counterexample":[{"expert":"relies on intuition and correctly identifies the verifiable outcome","outcome":"panel is less reliable by the problem's own outcome-based standard","panel":"rejects correct outcome due to stop-condition","setup":"A diagnostic checklist includes a mandatory stop-condition triggered whenever patient paperwork is incomplete. The patient's condition is nonetheless objectively verifiable (lab-confirmed). The panel rejects the diagnosis due to the stop-condition, missing the verifiable outcome."}],"target":"0382a66fd49784131a4953d05fe07ec8db320eb180105358f48dd718d77d2561"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target over-narrowly requires 'Cross-Item Sensitivity' (conditional interplay between checklist items) as the only genuine reliability gain. Counterexample: a well-calibrated additive checklist on a perfectly modular task (explicitly listed as a countercondition) can yield a genuine reliability gain over a single expert's intuition because independent non-experts applying each item reduce variance and the items individually map to verifiable outcome components. By excluding modular additive checklists from 'genuine' reliability, the target falsely demotes a genuine variance-cancellation mechanism to mere compliance.","counterexample":[{"cross_item_sensitivity":"absent","items":"additive and modular","reliability_gain":"genuine by outcome-based standard","setup":"A safety inspection with ten independent additive checks, each directly verifying a distinct objectively observable defect. A panel of non-experts applying each check achieves 95% agreement with ground truth; a single expert relying on intuition achieves 70%. The checklist has no conditional dependencies."}],"target":"4d39ca46694b5bc577b43509c7192d052d1b7a4ad0f8d753455307b97285d83b"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim mandates that changing a single checklist item to 'Fail' should logically propagate to the final judgment regardless of the 'obvious' answer, but this is a reliability test only if the checklist's logic is perfectly calibrated to the objective outcome. Counterexample: if the checklist item is a proxy indicator that can be false-positive (e.g., a symptom that is necessary in most but not all cases of the objectively verifiable condition), then a panel that blindly propagates the single 'Fail' to reject an actually-correct outcome is following a miscalibrated rule, not demonstrating reliability. Genuine reliability for objectively verifiable outcomes cannot require overriding the verifiable outcome whenever any one proxy item fails.","counterexample":[{"actual_reliable_prediction":"panel should judge Pass","objective_outcome":"verifiable Pass","setup":"A checklist item asks 'Is X present?', treated as mandatory for a 'Pass'. For a particular case, X is absent but the objective outcome is independently verifiable as Pass through other markers. A reliable panel should still judge Pass; the target's required propagation to 'Fail' would reduce reliability.","single_item_fail":"X absent","target_prediction":"panel must judge Fail"}],"target":"7cb04e5dfebea33e1aed28097872639cec4f6f5404403bcb7472b94de9d2543e"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim attributes the panel's superiority to 'cognitive variance cancellation' of uncorrelated stochastic errors via averaging, but a panel of non-experts following the same explicit checklist does not produce independent judgments; their errors are correlated by the checklist's common procedure. Counterexample: if the checklist is miscalibrated (its own listed countercondition), every non-expert following it makes the same systematic error, so there is no variance to cancel and averaging does not improve reliability over the expert's intuition. Thus the variance-cancellation mechanism cannot be the source of superiority in the very miscalibration case the target acknowledges; the genuine source would be the checklist's calibration, not error cancellation.","counterexample":[{"averaging_effect":"cancels nothing","individual_errors":"fully correlated via shared checklist","setup":"A checklist instructs non-experts to use a single proxy feature that is systematically biased for a subset of cases. Every non-expert applying it makes the same wrong judgment on those cases.","superiority_over_expert":"absent despite 'variance cancellation' claim"}],"target":"f6a68f5f31e26c2671ac6ce7e6a393e68fee55cd9ff693efb320b6d8e7491c9d"}]}